{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And its owner shall also be put to death.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory received (Mekhilta here; Sanhedrin 15a) that this is death by the hand of Heaven, and similar to it are \"the stranger who comes near shall be put to death\" (Numbers 18:7), \"they shall die in it, for they profane it\" (Leviticus 22:9). I have observed that the Torah does not use \"יוּמָת\" alone for those liable to death penalties by the court, but \"מוֹת יוּמָת\" is stated in all such cases. There is no objection from \"one who strikes a man shall be put to death\" (ibid. 24:21), nor from the Sabbath (Exodus 35:2), nor from a prophet who incites (Deuteronomy 13:6), for these have already been explained elsewhere. I do not know the reason for the translation of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 21:29), who rendered \"יִתְקְטִיל\" (shall be killed). Perhaps he means to say that he deserves to be executed, but there is a ransom upon him, or he intended to explain that when Scripture says \"and its owner shall also be put to death,\" it means: he shall be killed just as the man who was gored was killed — whether his day comes or he goes down into battle and perishes, HaShem will not spare him — this teaches that he is liable by the hand of Heaven to die by the hand of a killer, not his own death, in the manner of \"and I will kill you by the sword\" (Exodus 22:23). And Onkelos also rendered in the verse \"the stranger who comes near shall be put to death\" (Numbers 18:7) as \"יִתְקְטִיל,\" because he held according to the words of Rabbi Akiva, who said: the stranger who serves in the Sanctuary — Rabbi Akiva says: by strangulation (Sanhedrin 81a)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:18.693115840Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}